(329009, 23998, 3287, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all the comments, In hindsight I probably should have put this in portrait, but I''m very happy with 11th place. For everyone who wanted to know how this was done, I used my Sony DSC-S75 attached to a Scoptronix Maxview 40 eyepiece, attached to an Apex 90mm Mak spotting scope. I took about 12 photo, and this one was the sharpest.</td>', 'Thanks for all the comments, In hindsight I probably should have put this in portrait, but I''m very happy with 11th place. For everyone who wanted to know how this was done, I used my Sony DSC-S75 attached to a Scoptronix Maxview 40 eyepiece, attached to an Apex 90mm Mak spotting scope. I took about 12 photo, and this one was the sharpest.', '2003-06-18 10:13:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(329012, 24315, 656, '<td valign="top">great shot and well deserved win...</td>', 'great shot and well deserved win...', '2003-06-18 10:17:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(329019, 24338, 7743, '<td valign="top">I just had to comment after reading the rant thread.  In viewing the results for this challenge I found this entry to be one of the most interesting.  I really like the idea of using bubbles to capture detail in anything, even the American flag (no offense intended).  In this case the flag is not ''in your face'' and therefore non-imposing or intrusive.<br/> <br/>Well done.<br/> <br/></td>', 'I just had to comment after reading the rant thread.  In viewing the results for this challenge I found this entry to be one of the most interesting.  I really like the idea of using bubbles to capture detail in anything, even the American flag (no offense intended).  In this case the flag is not ''in your face'' and therefore non-imposing or intrusive. Well done. ', '2003-06-18 10:31:00.000000', NULL, 0),
